at::Tensor & cudnn_convolution_out(const at::Tensor & self, const at::Tensor & weight, at::IntArrayRef padding, at::IntArrayRef stride, at::IntArrayRef dilation, int64_t groups, bool benchmark, bool deterministic, bool allow_tf32, at::Tensor & out) {
  auto tmp_output = at::_ops::cudnn_convolution::call(self, weight, padding, stride, dilation, groups, benchmark, deterministic, allow_tf32);
    resize_out_helper(out, tmp_output);
  copy_arg(out, tmp_output);
  return out;
}
Currently, it internally uses cudnn_convolution that allocates a temporary output tensor, even when an output tensor is already provided, causing extra memory allocation and data copying. Functions like cudnn_batch_norm_backward_out, cudnn_affine_grid_generator_backward_out have the same behaviour.
Shall we directly compute the convolution results into the provided output tensor out, avoiding the creation of a temporary tensor and the subsequent copy operation?
Reducing memory overhead and unnecessary data movements should enhance performance, particularly in large-scale and performance-sensitive applications.
